  "raw_text": "Step 1: For the function \\( f(n) \\) to be continuous at \\( n = a \\), the left-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) must equal the right-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\), and both must equal \\( f(a) \\).\n\nStep 2: The left-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) from the left is given by the expression for \\( n < a \\), which is \\( 4n + 3 \\). So, we have:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to a^-}} f(n) = 4a + 3. \\]\n\nStep 3: The right-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) from the right is given by the expression for \\( n \\ge a \\), which is \\( 7n - 12 \\). So, we have:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to a^+}} f(n) = 7a - 12. \\]\n\nStep 4: Since the function is continuous at \\( n = a \\), these two limits must be equal:\n\\[ 4a + 3 = 7a - 12. \\]\n\nStep 5: Solve the equation \\( 4a + 3 = 7a - 12 \\) for \\( a \\):\n\\[ 4a + 3 = 7a - 12 \\]\n\\[ 3 + 12 = 7a - 4a \\]\n\\[ 15 = 3a \\]\n\\[ a = 5. \\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{5}<|im_end|>",
